)).  \n* \\(L_2\\) is vertical, hence **non\u2011sunny** (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \n* \\(L_3\\) is horizontal, hence **non\u2011sunny** (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \n\nAll three lines are distinct.\n\n---\n\n#### Point\u2011by\u2011point verification  \n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line(s)? | Check |\n|-----------------|------------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_1\\) (since \\(1=1\\)), \\(L_2\\) (since \\(x=1\\)), \\(L_3\\) (since \\(y=1\\)) | \u2713 |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_2\\) (vertical line \\(x=1\\)) | \\(x=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(x=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_3\\) (horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) | \\(y=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_1\\) (since \\(2=2\\)) | \u2713 |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(y=1\\) \u2713 |\n\nEvery point of \\(T_3\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines, satisfying the covering requirement.\n\n---\n\n#### Counting sunny lines  \n\nAmong \\(\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\) only \\(L_1\\) is sunny; the other two are non\u2011sunny.  \nHence the configuration contains **exactly one** sunny line.\n\n---\n\nSince a concrete configuration satisfying all conditions has been exhibited, the required integer \\(k=1\\) is indeed attainable for \\(n=3\\). \u220e"
