t parallel to any of  \n  \\(\\{x\\text{-axis},\\,y\\text{-axis},\\,x+y=0\\}\\).\n\nConsequently the remaining line (the **non\u2011sunny** line) is parallel to one\nof those three directions.  \nWe examine the three possibilities for the non\u2011sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Non\u2011sunny line horizontal: \\(y=c\\).\n\nThe possible values are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  The line \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Any line through two of these three points is either horizontal,\n  vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\); all are non\u2011sunny.  \n  Hence each of the three points would need its own sunny line, but only\n  two sunny lines are allowed \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  The line \\(y=2\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,3)\\).  \n\n  The only sunny line that can pass through two of them is the line through\n  \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\):\n  \\[\n  y=-2x+5\\qquad(\\text{slope }-2).\n  \\]\n  This covers \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\).  \n  The two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are collinear only on the\n  horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \n  Thus a third sunny line would be required \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  The line \\(y=3\\) contains \\((1,3)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,2),(2,2)\\) (five points).  \n\n  Compute the slopes of all pairs of these points:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n  (3,1)-(1,2) & -\\tfrac12\\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n  \\text{all other pairs} & 0,\\ \\infty,\\ \\text{or }-1\\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  No three points share a sunny slope, so any sunny line can contain at\n  most two of the five points.  Two sunny lines therefore cover at most\n  four points, leaving at least one uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\nHence no horizontal non\u2011sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Non\u2011sunny line vertical: \\(x=c\\).\n\nThe possible values are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  The line \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\) (three points).  \n\n  Every line through two of these points is horizontal, vertical, or of\n  slope \\(-1\\); all are non\u2011sunny.  Thus three sunny lines would be needed,\n  contradicting the requirement of exactly two.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  The line \\(x=2\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(3,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) (four points).  \n\n  The only sunny line among them is the line through \\((3,1)\\) and\n  \\((1,2)\\):\n  \\[\n  y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\qquad(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12).\n  \\]\n  This covers \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \n  The points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\),\n  which is non\u2011sunny, so a third sunny line would be required \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  The line \\(x=3\\) contains \\((3,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(2,1),(1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\) (five points).  \n\n  Sunny pairs among them are:\n  \\[\n  (1,1)-(2,2)\\ \\text{slope }1,\\qquad\n  (2,1)-(1,3)\\ \\text{slope }-2.\n  \\]\n  No three points share a sunny slope, so each sunny line can cover at most\n  two points.  Two sunny lines therefore cover at most four points, leaving\n  at least one uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\nThus no vertical non\u2011sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non\u2011sunny line of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nThe possible values are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  The line \\(x+y=2\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\) (five points).  \n\n  Sunny pairs are:\n  \\[\n  (1,2)