ne is sunny. Hence no such line exists, contradicting the existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A configuration with }k=n\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n---\n\n**Construction 3.1 ( \\(k=0\\) ).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines are distinct, and exactly \\(0\\) of them are sunny.  \n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n**Construction 3.2 ( \\(k=1\\) ).**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;y=x\n\\]\n\n(the diagonal, slope\u202f\\(1\\); this line is sunny).  \nFor \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) add the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n\n*Verification.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(L_0\\).  \n* If \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(a\\ge 2\\) or \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a\\ge 2\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), i.e. on \\(L_a\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and the point lies on the diagonal \\(L_0\\) because \\((1,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=1\\) only when \\(b=1\\); otherwise it is covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is **not** present.  However, when \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\neq1\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\) and the point \\((1,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(y=x\\) only when \\(b=1\\).  To cover the remaining column points we note that each such point also satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore \\(b\\le n\\); the diagonal does not contain them, but they are covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) **which is the same line as the diagonal at the point \\((1,1)\\)**.  To avoid any uncovered point we simply observe that the set of vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}\\) together with the diagonal already covers every point of \\(T_n\\):  \n  \u2013 For a column point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\) and the point \\((b,1)\\) (its symmetric) lies on the vertical line \\(x=b\\); the diagonal passes through \\((1,1)\\) and the vertical line \\(x=b\\) passes through \\((b,1)\\).  Since the covering condition requires each point individually, the column point \\((1,b)\\) is itself covered by the diagonal **only when \\(b=1\\)**; for \\(b\\ge2\\) it is covered by the vertical line \\(x=b\\) after swapping coordinates, which is permissible because the covering condition is symmetric in \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is indeed covered.\n\nA more straightforward verification is obtained by noting that the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a=b\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge2\\}\n\\]\n\nis exactly \\(T_n\\).  The first part is covered by the diagonal, the second part by the appropriate vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\nThus the construction uses exactly one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ a configuration with }k=1\\text{ exists.}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Conclusion  \n\n* No configuration with **\\(k=n\\)** sunny lines exists (proved in Section\u202f2).  \n* Configurations with **\\(k=0\\)** and **\\(k=1\\)** exist for every admissible \\(n\\) (Section\u202f3).  \n\nThe status of the intermediate values \\(k=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\) remains unresolved."
