            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write its equation as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c,\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Q,\\;m\\neq0,-1,\\;c\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q\\ge1\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\cap L\\) then \\(b=ma+c\\) and \\(a,b\\) are integers; hence  \n\n\\[\nqa-p a = q(c-b)\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nThus the integer abscissas occurring on \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(q\\); consequently at most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\) different values of \\(a\\) are possible.\n\nMoreover the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b=ma+c\\) yields  \n\n\\[\na+ma\\le n+1-c\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{1+|m|}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|m|\\ge1\\) (the cases \\(|m|=0\\) and \\(|m|=\\infty\\) are excluded), we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Necessary condition for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering requirement and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\n*Each non‑sunny line* (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)) can intersect the strip \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points, because for a fixed \\(x\\) (or \\(y\\) or \\(a+b\\)) the other coordinate can take at most \\(n\\) admissible values.\n\nUsing Lemma 1, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\ns:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, \\(n=2m\\) and \\(s=m\\); inserting into (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nkm+(n-k)2m\\ge m(2m+1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, \\(n=2m+1\\) and \\(s=m+1\\); (7) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk(m+1)+(n-k)(2m+1)\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le n .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus a **necessary** condition is  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that the bound (10) is also **sufficient**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.  The range \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (all \\(n\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-1). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) and therefore is sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(b-a=i-1\\) iff it lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nFor \\(j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\) define the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThese are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and hence non‑sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(b-a\\) is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(k-1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\,b-a+1}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines (11)–(12).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, as required. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) odd\n\nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \nConsider the following \\(n\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_0 &: y = x,\\\\[2mm]\nS_j &: y = -\\frac{1}{j}\\,x + (2m+2),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,m,\\\\[2mm]\nS_{m+j} &: y = -j\\,x + (2m+2),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,m .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every line in (13) is sunny.\n\n*Points on the lines.*  \nFor a fixed integer \\(c\\) with \\(-m\\le c\\le m\\) the line  \n\n\\[\ny = -c\\,x + (2m+2)\n\\]\n\ncontains the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\n(1,2m+1-c),\\qquad (2m+1-c,1),\n\\]\n\nand when \\(c=0\\) it also contains the central point \\((m+1,m+1)\\).  \nConsequently the \\(2m+1=n\\) lines (13) partition the whole set \\(T_n\\) into disjoint subsets of size \\(2\\) (for \\(c\\neq0\\)) and one singleton (for \\(c=0\\)).  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines, so the covering condition is fulfilled and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility when the bound is violated\n\nAssume that \\(n\\) is even and that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most \\(n/2\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > \\frac{n^{2}}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), not all points can be covered – a contradiction.  \nWhen \\(n\\) is odd, inequality (9) already forces \\(k\\le n\\); any larger \\(k\\) is impossible.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those described in (10).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines in a configuration satisfying the statement of the problem is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\); the previously missing cases \\(k=0\\) (three vertical lines) and \\(k=3\\) (the three sunny lines \\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+5,\\;y=-2x+5\\)) are now accounted for. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
