ullet.\n\n- **Inequality (7):**  \n  Since all \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, the inequality is necessary. The derivation is logically sound **provided** Lemma\u202f1\u2019s bound holds (which we have identified as a justification gap). Nonetheless, the algebraic manipulation from the bound to (7) is correct.\n\n**Result:** The necessary\u2011condition derivation is correct **conditional** on Lemma\u202f1; no new error introduced here.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Solving inequality (7) \u2013 obtaining the bound (10)\n\n> \u201cPut \\(s:=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n> If \\(n\\) is even, \\(n=2m\\) and \\(s=m\\); inserting into (7) gives  \n> \\[\n> km+(n-k)2m\\ge m(2m+1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-1 .\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]  \n> If \\(n\\) is odd, \\(n=2m+1\\) and \\(s=m+1\\); (7) becomes  \n> \\[\n> k(m+1)+(n-k)(2m+1)\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le n .\n> \\tag{9}\n> \\]  \n> Thus a **necessary** condition is  \n> \\[\n> k\\le\n> \\begin{cases}\n> n-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n> n,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n> \\end{cases}\n> \\tag{10}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n\n- **Even case:** Substituting \\(n=2m\\) and \\(s=m\\) into (7) yields  \n  \\[\n  km+(2m-k)2m\\ge m(2m+1).\n  \\]  \n  Simplifying gives \\(4m^{2}-km\\ge 2m^{2}+m\\), i.e. \\(2m^{2}-(k+1)m\\ge0\\). Since \\(m>0\\), this is equivalent to \\(k\\le2m-1=n-1\\). The algebra is correct.\n\n- **Odd case:** Substituting \\(n=2m+1\\) and \\(s=m+1\\) into (7) yields  \n  \\[\n  k(m+1)+(2m+1-k)(2m+1)\\ge (2m+1)(m+1).\n  \\]  \n  Simplifying leads to \\((2m+1)m\\ge km\\), i.e. \\(k\\le2m+1=n\\). The algebra is correct.\n\nThus the derived necessary condition (10) follows rigorously from (7).  \n\n**Result:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)  \n\n> \u201cLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n> *Sunny lines.* For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\; y = x + (i-1). \\tag{11}\n> \\]  \n> *Non\u2011sunny lines.* For \\(j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\) define the vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> V_j:\\; x=j . \\tag{12}\n> \\]  \n> *Verification.* Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n> * If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(b-a\\) is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(k-1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\,b-a+1}\\).  \n> * If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n> Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines (11)\u2013(12).\u201d\n\n**Verification of the construction:**\n\n- **Definition of the lines:**  \n  The sunny lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\) (which is indeed sunny). The vertical lines \\(V_j\\) are non\u2011sunny. No two lines coincide because the \\(L_i\\) have distinct intercepts and the \\(V_j\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates. So the set of \\(n\\) lines is distinct.\n\n- **Claim for points with \\(a\\le k\\):**  \n  The solution asserts that for any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le k\\) we must have \\(0\\le b-a\\le k-1\\). This is **false**.  \n  Counterexample (with \\(n=5,\\;k=2\\)):  \n  \\((a,b)=(1,5)\\) belongs to \\(T_5\\) because \\(1+5=6\\le6\\). Here \\(a=1\\le k\\) but \\(b-a=4\\), which is not in \\(\\{0,1\\}\\). Hence \\((1,5)\\) is not on any line \\(L_1\\) (\\(y=x\\)) or \\(L_2\\) (\\(y=x+1\\)). The vertical lines start at \\(x=3\\), so \\((1,5)\\) is uncovered.  \n\n  In general, for any \\(a\\le k\\) the quantity \\(b-a\\) can be as large as \\(n+1-2a\\), which may exceed \\(k-1\\). Therefore many points with small \\(a\\) are not covered by the proposed sunny lines.\n\n- **Conclusion:** The verification argument collapses; the construction does **not** cover all points of \\(T_n\\) for many values of \\(k\\). Consequently the claim that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable is **incorrect**.\n\n**Classification:** This is a **Critical Error** because the construction, which is the core of the sufficiency proof, is invalid. The error invalidates the claim that the bound (10) is sufficient for these values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd  \n\n> \u201cWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \n> Consider the following \\(n\\) sunny lines:  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> S_0 &: y = x,\\\\[2mm]\n> S_j &: y = -\\frac{1}{j}\\,x + (2m+2),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,m,\\\\[2mm]\n> S_{m+j} &: y = -j\\,x + (2m+2),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,m .\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\tag{13}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification of the lines:**\n\n- **Slope of \\(S_0\\):** \\(1\\) \u2013 sunny.\n\n- **Slope of \\(S_j\\) for \\(j\\ge2\\):** \\(-1/j\\) \u2013 this is not \\(0\\), not \\(\\infty\\), and not \\(-1\\); thus sunny.  \n\n- **Slope of \\(S_{m+j}\\) for any \\(j\\ge1\\):** \\(-j\\) \u2013 also sunny (unless \\(j=0\\), which does not occur).\n\n- **Problematic case \\(j=1\\) in the family \\(S_j\\):**  \n  For \\(j=1\\) we obtain the line \\(y = -x + (2m+2)\\), i.e. slope \\(-1\\). By definition a sunny line must **not** be parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)). Hence \\(S_1\\) is **non\u2011sunny**, contradicting the claim that \u201call \\(n\\) lines (13) are sunny\u201d.\n\n- **Coverage claim:** The solution states that each line contains either two points of \\(T_n\\) (or one for the central line) and that together they partition \\(T_n\\). While the partition argument is plausible (the lines are of the form \\(y = -c x + (2m+2)\\) with \\(c\\) ranging from \\(-m\\) to \\(m\\)), the presence of a non\u2011sunny line invalidates the construction for the problem\u2019s requirement that **exactly \\(k=n\\) lines are sunny**.\n\n**Classification:** This is a **Critical Error** \u2013 the construction includes a prohibited line (slope \\(-1\\)), so it does not satisfy the problem\u2019s conditions.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Impossibility when the bound is violated (even \\(n\\) and \\(k=n\\))\n\n> \u201cAssume that \\(n\\) is even and that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.  \n> By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contains at most \\(n/2\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of covered points is at most \\(n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2\\).  \n> Because \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > \\frac{n^{2}}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), not all points can be covered \u2013 a contradiction.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n\n- **Maximum points per sunny line for even \\(n\\):** Lemma\u202f1 (despite its flawed proof) asserts that any sunny line contains at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor = n/2\\) points when \\(n\\) is even. This bound is indeed correct (the line \\(y=x\\) attains \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) points).  \n\n- **Counting argument:** If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2} = \\frac{n^{2}}2\\). Since \\(|T_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) exceeds this for any \\(n\\ge3\\), a full covering is impossible. The reasoning is sound.\n\n**Result:** Correct (provided Lemma\u202f1\u2019s bound holds, which it does).  \n\n---\n\n### 8. Final conclusion  \n\n> \u201cThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those described in (10).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n\nThe conclusion relies on the sufficiency constructions for all admissible \\(k\\). As shown in Sections\u202f5 and\u202f6, those constructions are flawed (critical errors). Therefore the statement that the bound (10) is both necessary **and** sufficient is unsupported.  \n\n**Result:** The conclusion is **invalid** because the sufficiency part has not been correctly established.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment  \n\n- The **necessary\u2011condition** part of the solution (counting argument leading to (10)) is correct.  \n- **Lemma\u202f1** provides the essential bound but its proof contains a **justification gap** (incorrect handling of slopes with \\(|m|<1\\)).  \n- The **sufficiency** part is fundamentally flawed: the construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) does not cover all points, and the construction for \\(k=n\\) (odd \\(n\\)) includes a non\u2011sunny line. These are **critical errors** that invalidate the claimed answer.  \n\nConsequently, the solution does **not** correctly solve the problem."

