ach non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (2) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a configuration.\n\n4. **Explicit constructions for the extreme values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); all lines are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\) (possible only when \\(n\\) is odd).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \n   Consider the following \\(n\\) sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_0 &: y = x,\\\\[2mm]\n   L_j &: y = -\\frac{1}{j+1}\\,x + (2m+2),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,m,\\\\[2mm]\n   L_{m+j} &: y = -(j+1)\\,x + (2m+2),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,m .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   All slopes are rational and different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every line in (3) is sunny.  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(-m\\le c\\le m\\) the line  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -c\\,x + (2m+2)\n   \\]\n\n   (which appears among the lines (3)) contains exactly the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,2m+1-c),\\qquad (2m+1-c,1),\n   \\]\n\n   and when \\(c=0\\) it also contains the central point \\((m+1,m+1)\\).  \n   Hence the \\(2m+1=n\\) lines (3) partition the whole set \\(T_n\\) into disjoint subsets of size \\(2\\) (for \\(c\\neq0\\)) and one singleton (for \\(c=0\\)).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines, and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n5. **Impossibility when the bound (2) is violated.**  \n   If \\(n\\) is even and \\(k=n\\), then by Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contains at most \\(n/2\\) points 