            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny** (vertical, horizontal or parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll the \\(d_c\\) are non‑sunny and they are distinct.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for exactly one \\(c\\) in this range, hence belongs to the corresponding diagonal.  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\) cover \\(T_n\\) and contain **no** sunny line.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is feasible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nv_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(i\\le n-1\\) lie on one of the \\(v_i\\).  \nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any \\(v_i\\) is \\((n,1)\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\) when \\(a=n\\)).  \n\nChoose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for definiteness let  \n\n\\[\ns:\\;y=x-(n-1) .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(s\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(s\\) is sunny.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\{v_1,v_2,\\dots ,v_{n-1},s\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence \\(k=1\\) is feasible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nDenote by \\(U\\) the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on at least one non‑sunny line; let \\(|U|=u\\).  \nThe remaining points, \\(T_n\\setminus U\\), must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line and suppose it contains three distinct points  \n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nBecause the slope of \\(L\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), the three points have pairwise distinct\n\\(x\\)-coordinates and pairwise distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates.  \nIf two of the three points had the same sum \\(a_i+b_i\\), then the segment joining them would be parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the fact that \\(L\\) is not parallel to that diagonal.  \nHence the three sums \\(a_i+b_i\\) are also pairwise distinct.\n\nCollinearity of the three points gives\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nCross‑multiplying and rearranging yields\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1)(b_3-b_1)=(a_3-a_1)(b_2-b_1)\n\\Longrightarrow a_2b_3+a_3b_1+a_1b_2=a_3b_2+a_2b_1+a_1b_3 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nAdding the three equalities \\(a_i+b_i=c_i\\) (the distinct sums) to (3) and simplifying gives\n\\[\n(a_2-a_3)+(b_2-b_3)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a_2+b_2=a_3+b_3,\n\\]\nwhich contradicts the distinctness of the sums.  Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n---\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nEach non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\); the number of admissible \\(b\\) is \\(n+1-c\\le n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line.  \nA diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) contains the points \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots\\); its length is \\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\nHence the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Deriving the bound on \\(k\\)\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\;(n-k)n\\;+\\;2k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (4) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - n^2\\;+\\;2k\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\;\\le\\;1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is a non‑negative integer, (4) forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe constructions of Sections 1 and 2 show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) can indeed be realised, while the inequality derived in Section 4 proves that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0\\text{ and }1.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
