            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is vertical \\((x=\\text{const})\\), horizontal \\((y=\\text{const})\\) or parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. has slope \\(-1\\)).  \nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then the admissible values of \\(b\\) are \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.  \nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nFor a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the admissible points are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them. ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le s_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms, \\(q\\ge1\\).  \nBecause \\(L\\) is not vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), we have \\((p,q)\\neq(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\).  \n\nIf \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) are two distinct lattice points of \\(L\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}= \\frac{p}{q},\n\\]\n\nso \\(x_2-x_1\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\).  \nConsequently the \\(x\\)-coordinates of lattice points on \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression with step at least \\(q\\).  \n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le y\\le n\\).  \nHence the number of admissible \\(x\\)-values on \\(L\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nSince \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(q\\neq1\\) only when the slope is an integer different from \\(0,-1\\), the worst case (largest possible number of points) occurs for \\(q=1\\).  \nFor \\(q=1\\) the line has integer slope \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(y=px+b\\) implies  \n\n\\[\na+pa+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1-b}{p+1}\\le\\frac{n+1}{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(|p|\\ge2\\) forces \\(p+1\\ge3\\).  \nThus at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) integer points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on such a line. ∎\n\n\n\nLet a configuration contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nUsing Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 we obtain the covering inequality  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n\\;+\\;k\\,s_n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nRecall that  \n\n\\[\ns_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{2}, & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nInsert these values into (1).\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even:*  \n\n\\[\n(2t-k)2t+k\\,t\\ge t(2t+1)\n\\Longrightarrow 4t^{2}-2t k+t k\\ge 2t^{2}+t\n\\Longrightarrow 2t^{2}-t\\ge t k\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le 2t-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{2a}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd:*  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1-k)(2t+1)+k\\,(t+1)\\ge (2t+1)(t+1)\n\\Longrightarrow (2t+1)^{2}-k t\\ge (2t+1)(t+1)\n\\Longrightarrow 4t^{2}+4t+1-kt\\ge 2t^{2}+3t+1\n\\Longrightarrow t(2t+1-k)\\ge0\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le 2t+1=n .\n\\tag{2b}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n**(i)  The case \\(k=0\\).**  \nThe \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare distinct, non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is realizable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n**(ii)  Raising the number of sunny lines.**  \nAssume we already have a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(k\\) satisfies (3) and \\(k<n\\).  \nSelect a non‑sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal C\\) and pick a point \\(P\\in L\\cap T_n\\).  \n\nBecause the set of slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) is infinite, we can choose a slope \\(\\sigma\\) that\n\n* is not the slope of any line already present in \\(\\mathcal C\\), and  \n* is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nLet \\(L'\\) be the line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\sigma\\).  \n\\(L'\\) is sunny and distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered by a line other than \\(L\\) remains covered.  \nThe point \\(P\\) itself is now covered by \\(L'\\).  \nAll other points of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) are still covered because each such point lies on at least one *different* line of \\(\\mathcal C\\): a non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points, while \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\); consequently no non‑sunny line can be the *only* line covering any of its points.  \n\nTherefore replacing \\(L\\) by \\(L'\\) yields a new configuration \\(\\mathcal C'\\) that still covers \\(T_n\\) and has exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nStarting from the base configuration of (i) and applying the replacement step repeatedly, we obtain configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,\\dots ,K\\) sunny lines, where \\(K\\) is the l