+1,\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. a copy of \\(T_k\\) shifted right by \\(n-k\\).  Its cardinality is \\(|T_k'|=k(k+1)/2\\).\n\n   *Step C (covering the reduced triangle with sunny lines).*  \n   Because a sunny line can contain at most \\(s_k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k'\\) (Lemma 2 applied with \\(k\\) in place of \\(n\\)), the total capacity of \\(k\\) sunny lines is at least  \n   \\[\n   k\\;s_k\\;\\ge\\;k\\;\\frac{k+1}{2}= \\frac{k(k+1)}2 =|T_k'|.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Hence it is possible to cover all points of \\(T_k'\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   A concrete greedy algorithm provides such a covering:\n\n   * While some point of \\(T_k'\\) is still uncovered, pick any uncovered point \\(P\\).  \n   * Choose a rational slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(|q|\\ge 1\\) and \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) that has not been used before; this is always possible because there are infinitely many admissible slopes.  \n   * Let \\(L\\) be the unique line of that slope passing through \\(P\\).  \n   * By Lemma 2, \\(L\\) contains at most \\(s_k\\) points of \\(T_k'\\); add \\(L\\) to the family of sunny lines and delete all points of \\(L\\cap T_k'\\) from the uncovered set.\n\n   Because each new line removes at least one point and removes at most \\(s_k\\) points, after at most \\(k\\) iterations the uncovered set becomes empty (inequality (3) guarantees that we never need more than \\(k\\) lines).  The construction yields \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k'\\).\n\n   *Step D (final family).*  \n   The union of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (2) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained in Step C gives a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers the whole set \\(T_n\\) and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) is necessary, and the construction in §4 shows it is also sufficient.  Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the final answer.\n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is vertical \\((x=\\text{const})\\), horizontal \\((y=\\text{const})\\) or parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. has slope \\(-1\\)).  \nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (non‑sunny lines)  \n\nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then the admissible points are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nIf the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), its equation is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the points are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), at most \\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (sunny lines)  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le s_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(L\\) as  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\; q\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nwith the restriction \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (i.e. \\(q\\neq0\\), \\(p\\neq0\\), and \\(p\\neq -q\\)).  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in L\\cap T_n\\).  Because \\((a,b)\\) is an integer point, the numerator \\(p a+qb\\) must be divisible by \\(q\\); consequently the set of admissible \\(a\\)-coordinates on \\(L\\) forms an arithmetic progression whose step is exactly \\(q\\).  Hence the difference between the smallest and the largest admissible \\(a\\)-values is at most \\((\\#\\text{points}-1)q\\).\n\nFor any point of \\(T_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le b\\le n,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDefine the auxiliary quantity  \n\n\\[\nu:=a+b .\n\\]\n\nFrom the equation of the line we obtain  \n\n\\[\nu = a+b = a+\\frac{p}{q}a+b = \\frac{p+q}{q}\\,a + b .\n\\]\n\nThus, when we move from one integer point of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) to the next, the value of \\(u\\) changes by exactly \\(\\frac{|p+q|}{q}\\).  Because \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\neq -1\\), we have \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\); moreover \\(|p+q|=1\\) would force \\(\\frac{p}{q}=0\\) or \\(\\frac{p}{q}=-1\\), both excluded.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|p+q|\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the step in the sequence of admissible \\(u\\)-values is at least \\(2\\).  Since every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le u\\le n+1\\) (the lower bound follows from (4)), the number of distinct \\(u\\)-values that can appear on \\(L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nEach admissible \\(u\\) corresponds to at most one point of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) (because for a fixed \\(u\\) the equation \\(a+b=u\\) together with the line equation determines \\(a\\) uniquely).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = s_n .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A necessary inequality for \\(k\\)\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet \\(n-k\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.  \nUsing Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 we obtain the covering estimate  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\n   \\le (n-k)\\,n \\;+\\; k\\,s_n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) and the explicit value of \\(s_n\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(s_n=t\\).  Inequality (6) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2t(2t+1)}2 \\le (n-k)n + k t\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n2t^2+t \\le 4t^2-2t k + k t .\n\\]\n\nAfter simplification we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(s_n=t+1\\).  Inequality (6) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2 \\le (n-k)n + k(t+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n2t^2+3t+1 \\le 4t^2+2t -2t k + k(t+1) .\n\\]\n\nSimplifying gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) that satisfies (9).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The vertical backbone  \n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The reduced triangle  \n\nThe points that are **not** covered by the lines (10) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le k+1,\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a copy of \\(T_k\\) shifted right by \\(n-k\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_k'|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Covering \\(T_k'\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns_k:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 2 (applied with \\(k\\) in place of \\(n\\)), any sunny line meets \\(T_k'\\) in at most \\(s_k\\) points.  \nBecause of (11),\n\n\\[\nk\\,s_k\\;\\ge\\;k\\;\\frac{k+1}{2}=|T_k'|.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nHence it is possible to cover all points of \\(T_k'\\) with at most \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nWe now give an explicit greedy construction that uses **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n```\nInitialize   U := T_k'   (the set of uncovered points)\nInitialize   S := ∅      (the family of sunny lines)\n\nwhile U ≠ ∅ do\n      pick any point P = (a,b) ∈ U\n      choose a rational slope p/q with q≥1,\n             p/q ∉ {0,∞,−1} and p/q not used before;\n      let L be the unique line of slope p/q passing through P;\n      add L to S;\n      delete all points of L∩U from U;\nend while\n```\n\n*Why does the algorithm terminate after at most \\(k\\) iterations?*  \nEach iteration removes at least one point from \\(U\\) and removes **at most** \\(s_k\\) points (Lemma 2).  \nConsequently after \\(r\\) iterations we have removed at most \\(r\\,s_k\\) points.  \nIf the algorithm required more than \\(k\\) iterations, then after \\(k\\) iterations we would have removed at most \\(k\\,s_k\\) points, contradicting (12) because \\(|T_k'|>k\\,s_k\\) would remain uncovered.  Therefore the loop stops after at most \\(k\\) steps, and because we stop only when \\(U\\) becomes empty, it stops **exactly** after \\(k\\) steps.  The lines added are distinct (different slopes) and, by construction, each of them is sunny.\n\nThus we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal S\\) of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together cover the whole set \\(T_k'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  The final family  \n\nThe union  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the vertical lines cover the part with \\(x\\le n-k\\), the sunny family \\(\\mathcal S\\) covers the remaining part \\(T_k'\\)), and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(V_i\\) are non‑sunny, the lines of \\(\\mathcal S\\) are sunny).  Hence a configuration with the prescribed number \\(k\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nInequality (9) shows that no configuration can have more than the stated number of sunny lines.  \nThe construction of §2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (9) **is** attainable.  \nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{aligned}\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\;}\n\\]\n\n∎  "
