ed triangle’’ that remains after using \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.  \n   The reduced triangle contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points, whereas each sunny line can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   s_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   m,   & k=2m\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   m+1, & k=2m+1\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence with an even \\(k\\) the total capacity of the \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_k = 2m\\cdot m = \\frac{k^2}{2}\n   <\\frac{k(k+1)}2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which is insufficient to cover the reduced triangle.  \n   Consequently **even \\(k>0\\) is impossible**.  \n\n3. **Sufficiency for admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   *Step A (vertical backbone).*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Step B (reduced triangle).*  \n   The uncovered points form  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le k+1,\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a copy of \\(T_k\\) shifted right by \\(n-k\\).  Its size is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k'|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step C (covering \\(T_k'\\) when \\(k\\) is odd).*\n   For odd \\(k=2m+1\\) we have \\(s_k=m+1\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_k = (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 =|T_k'|.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines must together contain **exactly** \\(|T_k'|\\) points, i.e. each of them must contain precisely \\(s_k\\) points and the families must be pairwise disjoint.\n\n   A greedy algorithm produces such a family:\n\n   1. Initialise the uncovered set \\(U:=T_k'\\) and the family \\(\\mathcal S:=\\varnothing\\).  \n   2. While \\(U\\neq\\varnothing\\):  \n      * pick any point \\(P\\in U\\);  \n      * choose a rational slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), \\(q\\ge1\\), and \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) that has not been used before