ed right by \\(n-k\\).  \n   Its cardinality is \\(|T_k'|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n\n   For a sunny line inside this triangle Lemma 2 gives the sharper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap T_k'|\\le s_k:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=m .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the total number of points that the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_k = 2m\\cdot m = \\frac{k^{2}}{2}\n   <\\frac{k(k+1)}2 =|T_k'|,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  \n   Consequently **no even \\(k>0\\) can occur**.\n\n4. **Construction for admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be odd, \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge0\\)).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – vertical backbone.*  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the \\(V_i\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – reduced triangle.*  \n   The points not yet covered are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le k+1,\\;a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   which is a translate of \\(T_k\\).  Its size is \\(|T_k'|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – partition of \\(T_k'\\) into \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(-m\\le t\\le m\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = t\\,x + (m+1-t).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_t\\) is \\(t\\); because \\(|t|\\le m\\) and \\(m\\ge0\\), we have \\(t\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the line is not vertical, so each \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \n   The \\(k=2m+1\\) values of \\(t\\) give exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   **Claim.**  For every \\(t\\) the set \\(L_t\\cap T_k'\\) consists of exactly \\(m+1\\) points, and the families \\(\\{L_t\\cap T_k'\\}_{t=-m}^{m}\\) are pairwise disjoint; their union is \\(T_k'\\).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Write a point of \\(T_k'\\) as \\((x,y)\\) with \\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1,\\;x+y\\le 2m+2\\).  \n   Solving (7) for \\(y\\) gives \\(y = t x + (m+1-t)\\).  \n   Substituting into the inequality \\(x+y\\le 2m+2\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   x + t x + (m+1-t) \\le 2m+2\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   (1+t)x \\le m+1+t .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(|t|\\le m\\), the right‑hand side of (8) is positive, and the inequality forces  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x\\le m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   For each admissible integer \\(x\\) the corresponding \\(y\\) given by (7) is automatically an integer and satisfies \\(y\\ge1\\) (by (8)) and \\(x+y\\le 2m+2\\) (again by (8)).  \n   Hence for a fixed \\(t\\) the admissible values of \\(x\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,2,\\dots ,m+1,\n   \\]\n\n   giving precisely \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_k'\\) on \\(L_t\\).  \n\n   If a point \\((x,y)\\) belongs to both \\(L_{t_1}\\) and \\(L_{t_2}\\) with \\(t_1\\neq t_2\\), then from (7) we would have  \n\n   \\[\n   t_1x+(m+1-t_1)=t_2x+(m+1-t_2)\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   (t_1-t_2)x = t_1-t_2,\n   \\]\n\n   whence \\(x=1\\).  Substituting \\(x=1\\) into (7) gives \\(y=m+1\\), which is the unique point common to all the lines.  However this point occurs **only when** \\(t=0\\); for any \\(t\\neq0\\) the formula (7) yields a different \\(y\\).  Consequently the only possible common point would be \\((1,m+1)\\), but this point lies on \\(L_0\\) and on no other \\(L_t\\) because for \\(t\\neq0\\) we have \\(y=t\\cdot1+(m+1-t)=m+1\\) only when \\(t=0\\).  Hence the intersections are empty; the families are disjoint.  \n\n   Finally, counting gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{t=-m}^{m}|L_t\\cap T_k'| = (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 =|T_k'|,\n   \\]\n\n   so the union of the \\(L_t\\cap T_k'\\) is exactly \\(T_k'\\). ∎  \n\n   *Step 4 – final family.*  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid -m\\le t\\le m\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the \\(L_t\\)’s).  \n   By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) either lies on a vertical line \\(V_i\\) (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\)) or, after the shift by \\(n-k\\), belongs to \\(T_k'\\) and therefore lies on one of the sunny lines \\(L_t\\).  Hence **all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered**.\n\n5. **Putting necessity and sufficiency together.**  \n\n   *Necessity* – inequality (4) together with the parity obstruction (even \\(k>0\\) impossible) shows that any feasible \\(k\\) must be either \\(0\\) or an odd integer not exceeding the bound in (4).  \n\n   *Sufficiency* – the explicit construction of §4 works for every such \\(k\\).  \n\n   Therefore the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
