nce every feasible \\(k>0\\) must be odd.\n\n3. **Construction for admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k=2m+1\\) be odd (\\(m\\ge0\\)).  \n\n   *Vertical backbone.*  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Reduced triangle.*  \n   The remaining points are  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le k+1,\\ a\\ge1,\\ b\\ge1\\},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   a translate of \\(T_k\\) and of size \\(|T_k'|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(-m\\le t\\le m\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\;y=t\\,x+(m+1-t).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(t\\) satisfies \\(t\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the line is not vertical, so every \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \n   There are exactly \\(k=2m+1\\) such lines.\n\n   **Verification that the \\(L_t\\) cover \\(T_k'\\) point‑by‑point.**  \n   Let \\((x,y)\\in T_k'\\). Because \\(x+y\\le 2m+2\\) we can write  \n\n   \\[\n   y = t x + (m+1-t)\n   \\]\n\n   for a unique integer \\(t\\) in the interval \\([-m,m]\\): solving for \\(t\\),\n\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{y-(m+1)}{x-1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(x\\ge1\\) and \\(y\\ge1\\), the numerator and denominator are integers and the inequality \\(x+y\\le2m+2\\) guarantees \\(-m\\le t\\le m\\).  \n   Consequently each point of \\(T_k'\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_t\\).\n\n   Moreover, for a fixed \\(t\\) the admissible values of \\(x\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,m+1\\); substituting these into (4) yields the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,\\,t x+m+1-t),\\qquad x=1,\\dots ,m+1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. exactly \\(m+1\\) points on \\(L_t\\).  \n   The families \\(\\{L_t\\cap T_k'\\}_{t=-m}^{m}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and their total cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_k'|,\n   \\]\n\n   so their union is precisely \\(T_k'\\).\n\n   *Putting everything together.*  \n   Define  \n\n 